growing tensions between china and  australia were laid bare with this tweet  of a fake image depicting an australian  soldier  holding a knife to the throat of an  afghan child  alluding to a recent report on alleged  war crimes  by some australians what followed was a  war of words  with beijing refusing to apologize and  the stage-backed global times newspaper  doubling down with another cartoon of  australian soldiers  at the moment it appears that both sides  are not willing to stand back  and both sides are quite willing to  escalate  it's been escalating for years australia  has legislated over concern that china  was too involved in politics here  and it became the first country to ban  chinese tech giant huawei  from its 5g network it's also spoken out  about china's handling of hong kong  and the coronavirus beijing hit back  with bans and tariffs on australian  industries clearly  australia is taking economic punishment  for uh taking a political stance that  china does not agree with  now australia is taking beijing to the  world trade organization  threatening legal action over its  tariffs on bali  allies have rallied behind australia  with campaigns to buy australian  products  australian politicians have  traditionally taken a bipartisan  approach to china but in a rare divide  on foreign policy  the opposition labour party has accused  the prime minister of presiding  over a complete breakdown of  relationships  the government is now hoping for a reset  the question is how to reset relations  when china isn't answering calls  businesses caught up in the tensions are  hoping for a diplomatic resolution  in terms of immediate remedies or  fantasies there are none i don't think  but what we have been saying to  government is that we do need a reset  we do need to focus on you know working  through the challenges  the challenge will be navigating a path  between voicing what australia believes  to be legitimate concerns  while maintaining lucrative markets and  also  opening new ones nicola gage al jazeera  canberra
 

